## DESCRIPTION
## Find the length of two segments of a biathlon
## ENDDESCRIPTION

## KEYWORDS('application','linear','biathlon')

## DBsubject(Linear algebra)
## DBchapter(Systems of linear equations)
## DBsection(Applications)
## Date(September 5, 2008)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Peter Staab)

# updated 2023.11.10

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'randomPerson.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

do {
    $runrate  = list_random(4,  5,  6);
    $bikerate = list_random(12, 15, 20);

    $runlength  = random(6,  10);
    $bikelength = random(15, 25);

    $total = $runlength + $bikelength;

    $time = 60 * ($runlength / $runrate + $bikelength / $bikerate);
} while ($time % 5 == 0);

$hrs  = floor($time / 60);
$mins = $time % 60;

$p = randomPerson();

BEGIN_PGML

[$p] performs in a biathlon, an event with running and biking. The combined 
distance is [$total] miles. [$p->They] can bike [$bikerate] miles per hour and run [$runrate]
miles per hour. If [$p->they] [$p->verb('finish')] in [$hrs] hours and [$mins]
minutes, how long is each leg of the event?

- Running length: [_]{Real($runlength)} miles
- Biking length: [_]{Real($bikelength)} miles   
END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
First of all, let [`x`] be the number of miles spent running and [`y`] miles spent biking.

The total distance is just the sum of the two, so the first equation is
[``` x+y=[$total] ```]
And the total time is the sum of the time spent running and biking where time = distance/rate.
[``` \frac{x}{[$runrate]} + \frac{y}{[$bikerate]} = \frac{[$time]}{60} ```]
where [$hrs] hours and [$mins] minutes is converted to hours.

The solution is found numerous ways.  One standard way is to write these two equation 
in augmented matrix form or
[``` \left[ \begin{array}{rr|r} 1 & 1 & [$total] \\
\frac{1}{[$runrate]} & \frac{1}{[$bikerate]} & \frac{[$time]}{60} 
\end{array} \right] ```]
and then using Gauss-Jordon to reduce it to 
[``` \left[ \begin{array}{rr|r} 1 & 0 & [$runlength] \\
0 & 1 & [$bikelength] 
\end{array} \right] ```]
showing that the running leg was [$runlength] miles long and the biking leg was
[$bikelength] miles long.
END_PGML_SOLUTION


ENDDOCUMENT();
